          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            String field theory is supposed to be something like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                    field theory
                  %th
                    of fundamental
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
                  %th
                    and their
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
                  %th
                    which in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
                    is given by
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
          %p
            Recall that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is a higher dimensional version of the Feynman
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em String field theory
            is the attempt to identify this Lagrangian description corresponding to the string perturbation series.
          %p So string field theory is the attempt to complete the following analogy:
          %p Feynman perturbation series : QFT Lagrangian :: String perturbation theory : String field theory .
          %h3#motivation Motivation
          %p The original hope was that string field theory would be a way to embed the string perturbation series prescription into a more coherent non-perturbative framework.
          %h3#Achievements Achievements
          %p
            The most detailed insight that has come out of the study of string field theory is the full understanding of the role of the “tachyon” field in bosonic perturbative string theory. In the bosonic version of the theory one of the excitations of the string is a quantum that appears to have imaginary mass. Such “tachyonic” quanta appear in ordinary field theory when the perturbation series is developed around an extremum of the QFT action functional that is not a local minimum, but a local maximum: it indicates that the classical configuration around which the perturbation series computes the quantum corrections is dynamically unstable and time evolution will tend to evolve it to the next local minimum. In the perturbative quantum description the movement to the next local minimum manifests itself in the
            %em condensation
            (as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bose-Einstein+condensate"} Bose-Einstein condensation
            = succeed "." do
              %em tachyon condensation
          %p Shortly after its conception it was suspected that the tachyon that appears in the perturbation theory of the bosonic string is similarly an indication that the bosonic string’s perturbation series has to be understood as being a perturbation about a local maximum of some action functional. String field theory aimed to provide that notion of action functional. And indeed, in bosonic string field theory one has a kind of higher action functional and may compute the “tachyon potential” that it implies. It indeed has a local maximum at the point about which the ordinary bosonic string perturbation series is a perturbative expansion, while a local minimum is foun nearby.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ashoke+Sen"} Ashoke Sen
            conjectured the statement – now known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sen%27s+conjecture"} Sen's conjecture
            – that the depth of this tachyon potential, i.e. the energy density difference between this local maximum and this local minimum corresponds precisely to the energy density of the space-filling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D25-brane"} D25-brane
            that is seen in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
          %p The detailed quantitative confirmation of Sen’s conjecture has been one of the main successes of string field theory. In the course of this a detailed algebraic description of the “true closed bosonic string vacuum”, i.e. of the theory at that local tachyon potential minimum has been found. However, the algebraic expressions involved tend to be hard to handle in their complexity.
          %p
            There are numerical indications that indeed as the D25-brane decays, the remaining vacuum contains (only) closed strings. See the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BosonicStringVacuumAndSenConjecture"} references below
          %h3#shortcomings Shortcomings
          %p The shortcoming of the current development of string field theory can probably be summarized as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it has been studied as a theory of a
                %em classical
                action functional. Little is known about the true quantum effects of the string field theory action functional.
            %li
              %p the best understanding exists for bosonic open string field theory, while closed and supersymmetric string field theory has remained much less accessible.
          %h3#in_terms_of_higher_category_theory In terms of higher category theory
          %p
            Closed string field theory is governed by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
            of interactions, open string field theory by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+algebra"} A-infinity algebra
            and open-closed string field theory by a mixture of both: an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open-closed+homotopy+algebra"} open-closed homotopy algebra
          %h2#bosonic_open_string_field_theory Bosonic open string field theory
          %p (…)
          %h2#bosonic_closed_string_field_theory Bosonic closed string field theory
          %p
            So far string field theory is defined in terms of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} quantum master action
            is known, but apart from that not much detail about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of this action has been considered in the literature.
          %h3#the_interaction_terms The interaction terms
          %p
            The unrestricted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of string field theory is the subcomplex of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            of the closed (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"}> super
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"}> string
            , regarded as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector space
            with respect to the ghost number grading, on those elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            that satisfy
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 0
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b_0 - \bar b_0) \Psi = 0
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn 0
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_0 - \bar L_0) \Psi = 0
                (“level matching condition”),
          %p
            We shall write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            for this graded vector space. See (
            %a{:href => "#Markl"}> Markl, section 1
            )
          %p
            This is equipped for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            with a
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,\cdots,-]_k : \mathfrak{g}^{\otimes k} \to \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} (k+1)-point function
            of the string (the amplitude for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            closed strings coming in and merging into a single outgoing string). For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi BRST
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-]_1 = d_{BRST}
                  \\,.
          %p
            These operations are graded-symmetric: for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Psi_j\}
            of homogeneous degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi deg
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg \Psi_j
            and for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq i \lt k
            we have
          #eq:GradedSymmetrykBracket.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\Psi_1 , \cdots, \Psi_i, \Psi_{i+1}, \cdots, \Psi_k]_k
                  \=
                  (-1)^{(deg \Psi_i)(deg \Psi_{i+1})}
                  [\Psi_1 , \cdots, \Psi_{i+1}, \Psi_{i}, \cdots, \Psi_k]_k .
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach, (4.4)
            ).
          %p
            Moreover, there is a bilinear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle -,- \rangle : \mathfrak{g} \otimes \mathfrak{g} \to \mathbb{C}
          %p
            coming from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            inner product of string states (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach (2.60)
            ). This is non-degenerate on elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            which are annihilated by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} ghost
            operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                    %mo −
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  b_0^- := b_0 - \bar b_0
          %p
            in that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathfrak{g}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                    %mo −
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_0^- A = 0
            we have
          #eq:NonDegeneracyOfInnerProduct.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\forall B \in \mathfrak{g} \,:\,
                  \\langle A,B\rangle = 0)
                  \\Rightarrow
                  A = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (2.61)
            ).
          %p
            The inner product satisfies for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi_1, \Psi_2
            of homogeneous degree the relation
          #eq:GradedSymmetryBilinearPairing.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle \Psi_1 , \psi_2 \rangle
                  \=
                  (-1)^{(deg \Psi_1 + 1) (deg \Psi_2 + 1)}
                  \\langle \Psi_2, \Psi_1 \rangle
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (2.50)
            ).
          %p
            Moreover, it is non-vanishing only on pairs of elements of total degree 5. (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (2.31)(2.44)
            ).
          %p
            From this one constructs the
            = succeed "-point" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          #eq:CorrelationFunctions.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{ \Psi_0, \Psi_1, \cdots, \Psi_k \}
                  \:=
                  \\langle \Psi_0, [\Psi_1, \cdots, \Psi_k]_k \rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            These are still graded-symmetric in all arguments: for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Psi_j\}
            of homogeneous degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi deg
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg \Psi_j
            and all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq i \lt k
            we have
          #eq:GradedSymmetrykPointFunction.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi deg
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\Psi_0, \cdots, \Psi_i, \Psi_{i+1}, \cdots, \Psi_k\}
                  \=
                  (-1)^{(deg \Psi_i)(deg \Psi_{i+1})}
                  \\{\Psi_0, \cdots, \Psi_{i+1}, \Psi_{i}, \cdots, \Psi_k\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (4.36)
            ).
          %h3#the_action_functional The action functional
          #LabelConfigurationSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The proper
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
              of string field theory is the sub-complex of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
              of the closed (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"}> super
              \-)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
              on those elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
              for which
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 0
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b_0 - \bar b_0) \vert\Psi\rangle = 0
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn 0
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_0 - \bar L_0) \vert \Psi \rangle = 0
                  (“level matching condition”);
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi Ψ
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo †
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \Psi \rangle^\dagger = - (\langle \Psi \vert)
                  (“reality”);
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert \Psi\rangle
                  is Grassmann even (…define…)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ghostnumber
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo =
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ghostnumber \vert \Psi \rangle = 2
                  (…define…)
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (3.9)
            )
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of closed string field theory is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ↦
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ψ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \Psi
                  \\mapsto
                  \\sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                  \\frac{1}{(k+1)!}
                  \\langle \Psi, [\Psi, \cdots, \Psi]_k\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (4.41)
            )
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi BRST
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-]_1 = d_{BRST}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST operator
            this starts out as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi BRST
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ψ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \Psi
                  \=
                  \\frac{1}{2}\langle \Psi , d_{BRST} \Psi \rangle
                  +
                  \\frac{1}{3} \langle \Psi, [\Psi, \Psi]_2\rangle
                  +
                  \\cdots
                  \\,.
          %p
            This immediately gives the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            by variation with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (4.46)
            ). The analogous statement for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            in in (
            %a{:href => "#Sen15"}> Sen 15 (2.22)
            ).
          %p
            (See also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/string+theory+FAQ#WhatAreTheEquations"} string theory FAQ – What are the equations of string theory?
          %h3#AsAnInfinityCSTheory
            As an
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The above action functional for closed string field theory turns out to have a general abstract meaning in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
            in that it arises precisely as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+element"} Chern-Simons element
            of the binary pairing regarded as a binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
            of string fields.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The string
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
              equipped with its
              = succeed "-ary" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathfrak{g}, \{[-,\cdots,-]_k\})
            %p
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} L-∞ algebra
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Zwiebach93"}> Zwiebach 93, (4.12)
            ). For more details on the
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %em
              %a{:href => "ReferencesHomotopyAlgebra"}> References – Relation to L-∞- and A-∞-algebra
              )
            \.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The inner product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
              satisfies the definition of a non-degenerate
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
              on this
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LabelConfigurationSpace"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              For simplicity of notation we discuss this as if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              were finite-dimensional. The argument for the infinite-dimensional case follows analogously.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_a\}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              with dual basis
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msup
                        %mi t
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t^a\}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              is generated from the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t^a\}
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi CE
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{CE(\mathfrak{g})}
                    \:
                    t^a
                    \\mapsto
                    \-
                    \\sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                    \\frac{1}{k!} [t_{a_1}, \cdots, t_{a_k}]_k \,\,
                    t^{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge t^{a_k}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(\mathfrak{g})
              is similarly generated from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t^a, r^a\}
              with differential
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{W(\mathfrak{g})}
                    \:
                    t^a
                    \\mapsto
                    \-
                    \\sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                    \\frac{1}{k!} [t_{a_1}, \cdots, t_{a_k}] \,\,
                    t^{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge t^{a_k}
                    + r^a
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi a
                    %mo ↦
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 0
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d_{W(\mathfrak{g})}
                    \:
                    r^a
                    \\mapsto
                    \\sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                    \\frac{1}{k!}
                    [t_{a_0}, t_{a_1}, \cdots, t_{a_k}]_{k+1}
                    \\,
                    r^{a_0} \wedge t^{a_1} \wedge \cdots \wedge t^{a_k}
                    \\,.
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P_{a b} := \langle t_a, t_b\rangle
            %p
              for the components of the bilinear pairing in this basis. By
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GradedSymmetryBilinearPairing"} (3)
              it follows that we can indeed regard
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P_{a b} r^a \wedge r^b
                    \\in W(\mathfrak{g})
            %p
              as an element in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+algebra"} Weil algebra
              (since
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi deg
                      %msup
                        %mi r
                        %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi deg
                      %msup
                        %mi t
                        %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg r^a = deg t^a + 1
            %p
              Therefore to see that this is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
              it remains to check that it is
              = succeed "-closed." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_W
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C_{a_0, \cdots, a_k} :=
                    \\{t_{a_0}, \cdots, t_{a_k}\}
            %p
              for the components of the
              = succeed "-point" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CorrelationFunctions"} (4)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mrow
                                %mi W
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi 𝔤
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mi P
                              %mrow
                                %mi a
                                %mi b
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mi a
                            %mo ∧
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mi b
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mn 2
                            %msub
                              %mi P
                              %mrow
                                %mi a
                                %mi b
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mi a
                            %mo ∧
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %munderover
                                %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                                %mrow
                                  %mi k
                                  %mo =
                                  %mn 1
                                %mn ∞
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mi ⋯
                              %mo ,
                              %msub
                                %mi t
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mi k
                              %msub
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                                %mi k
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %msup
                                %mi r
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mn 1
                              %mo ∧
                              %msup
                                %mi t
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mn 2
                              %mo ∧
                              %mi ⋯
                              %mo ∧
                              %msup
                                %mi t
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi a
                                    %mi k
                              %mo )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mn 2
                            %munderover
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                              %mrow
                                %mi k
                                %mo =
                                %mn 1
                              %mn ∞
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 0
                                %mo ,
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo ,
                                %mi ⋯
                                %mo ,
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mi k
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 0
                            %mo ∧
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 1
                            %mo ∧
                            %msup
                              %mi t
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 2
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi ⋯
                            %mo ∧
                            %msup
                              %mi t
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    d_{W(\mathfrak{g})} P_{a b } r^a \wedge r^b
                    \&amp; =
                    2 P_{a b} r^a \wedge
                    \\left(
                    \\sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                    [t_{a_1}, \cdots, t_{a_k}]_k
                    \\,
                    r^{a_1} \wedge t^{a_2}\wedge \cdots \wedge t^{a_k}
                    \\right)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    2 \sum_{k = 1}^\infty
                    C_{a_0, a_1, \cdots, a_k}
                    \\,
                    r^{a_0} \wedge r^{a_1} \wedge t^{a_2} \wedge \cdots \wedge t^{a_k}
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This expression vanishes term-by-term by the symmetry properties
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GradedSymmetrykPointFunction"} (5)
              when restricted to fields of even degree: by first switching the factors in the wedge product and then relabelling the indices we obtain
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 0
                                %mo ,
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo ,
                                %mi ⋯
                                %mo ,
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mi k
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 0
                            %mo ∧
                            %msup
                              %mi r
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi deg
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi a
                                      %mn 0
                                %mo +
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi deg
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi a
                                      %mn 1
                                %mo +
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo +
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi deg
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi a
                                      %mn 0
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi deg
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi a
                                      %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
